  },
  "09": {
   "hash": "f182fe1854e54c52",
   "takes": [
    "d68b768a08e29d7518ae",
    "e3790de55e3f13ea6bab"
   ],
   "duration": 15.809,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "One real, widely used consensus implementation's own documentation states this exact purpose directly, in its own words.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 9.558
    },
    {
     "text": "It names stopping a partitioned node from disrupting the cluster on rejoin, directly.",
     "start": 9.978,
     "end": 15.459
    }
   ],
   "lufs": -16.5,
   "true_peak": -1.9,
   "wpm": 114,
   "wer": 0.0,
   "exact": true,
   "tolerated": []
  },
  "10": {
   "hash": "7168bf4470f9a8bd",
   "takes": [
    "db4a9287b31b9915d3dd",
    "d323a27cba1b46ce64ef",
    "e083ddf29af8e686424b"
   ],
   "duration": 33.233,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "So here's the real shape of this whole topic: timing alone picks a candidate, but two separate guardrails decide whether that pick is actually safe.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 10.671
    },
    {
     "text": "One guardrail looks backward, at whether a candidate's own history is caught up; the other looks forward, refusing to even spend a higher term until winning is already likely.",
     "start": 11.091,
     "end": 22.926
    },
    {
     "text": "Neither guardrail needs a clock anyone trusts, or a human watching; both come straight out of comparing plain numbers a node already has on hand.",
     "start": 23.346,
     "end": 32.883
    }
   ],
